  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-160942",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14128542/assets/0412da40e0e04e8da5ec92db1ad665251209a718333ec008ceeebbe308c71855.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=0$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(0\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,0\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(0,\\,0),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14128542/assets/32aa42ca7dc665fb0a486f9a4f984916857d3964934c7a74c50723f1018fd833.png)",
